to  try to soften ukraine's negotiating  position that's why i'm skeptical that  you know uh you know and i agree with my  uh  colleagues here who have said that this  is still time to go to fully play out  and keep in mind there's another thing a  very important date is coming up may 9th  which is the victory date uh for for  russia and the soviet union's victory  over the nazi germany and so vladimir  putin has been pulling troops uh into  ukraine's theater of action from armenia  from georgia from tajikistan even from  the far east we've also heard uh reports  that syrian fighters and hezbollah  fighters who may be coming to ukraine it  shows you the desperation that vladimir  putin has in order to show some sort of  a victory to uh his people i mean he's  lost so much uh blood and treasure  already that anything short of some kind  of a um  visible victory will be tantamount to  his political debt that's why i think  this is like a do or die moment for  vladimir putin he is very much concerned  with his own political survival he's  even physical survival i would say and  so i think he's we might yet see much  more escalation and much more misery uh  before there is serious talk of a peace  agreement andre kortunov in other  interviews with you you've said that  it's hard for you to comprehend mr  putin's actions when it comes to ukraine  are you any closer to understanding them  and do you think that there are any  international actors out there any  interlocutors who could offer mr putin a  credible off-ramp to the conflict at  this stage  well first of all let me say that i  agree with peter that uh  putin and the russian leadership cannot  afford to lose they need a victory  uh even symbolic victory are even not  complete victory but something that can  represent it uh domestically as a  victory and this is important uh in  terms of uh a foreign  engagement i think that there are  not too many people uh  in the world uh who might uh influence  uh the russian leadership uh  maybe a chairman c zhang bin from china  uh can be regarded as an honest broker  because china has good relations with  russia but it also has good relations  with ukraine it's a major trading  partner of both countries it's a major  investor in ukraine  so i can imagine that some kind of  mediation services from china  could be received positively  in moscow i am less confident about uh  attempts to mediate taken by people like  the turkish president  erdogan  because erdogan  turkey is a member to the north atlantic  alliance erdogan has never  tried to hide uh his sympathy uh uh for  uh ukraine uh and uh uh i don't think  that uh he can be regarded uh in the  kremlin uh as a as a mediator as an  honest broker  uh there might be some other people but  there are clearly not too many of them  and i think that the task of mediation  will not be easy  zachary pakin let me ask you i mean from  your vantage point who can be the most  effective mediators going forward  i don't think there's an obvious answer  to the question as andre just said there  are a number of you know theoretical uh  you know individuals or countries out  there that you know for one reason or  another particularly because they have  good relations with with both russia and  ukraine could serve that role the  question is do they actually have you  know the political will and capacity to  do so so we talked about china having  good relations with both we've talked  about turkey being both a member of nato  but having also you know pursued  pragmatic relations with russia in  recent years you know carving out  regional compacts in places like syria  and the south caucasus and in libya uh  you know people have also talked about  israel as being a possibility uh but at  the end of the day each of them has  their own narrow interests as well which  make it very difficult for them to be  able to you know break out of the  current geopolitical stalemate right  china's a very good example of this  uh you know on the on the one hand you  know china does have its interest in  maintaining good ties with russia for  geopolitical reasons in the sort of  great game that is now emerging with the  united states but china you know is  still a rising power in some respects  and needs to maintain you know good  relations uh you know with uh with the  united states and and uh you know with  the broader western political community  economically speaking technologically  speaking in order to continue that rise  so china's in a difficult position of  needing to pursue its own uh narrow  interests and that's naturally going to  make china a little bit more cautious  and because of the good relationship  that china and russia have developed and  invested in over recent years i think  that xi jinping is also going to be  quite cautious about you know  potentially uh twisting putin's arm you  know and trying to get him to bring this  war to an end and that would require  using not just carrots but also sticks  right that would involve exerting  political capital i don't think china's  ready to do that i think that's  unfortunate because i think that  countries like china india is another  example this could be an opportunity for  asia really to have its moment in the  sun and to surpass sort of europe and to  inaugurate the post-western world but  because of you know these various  dynamics that i've just laid out that  makes things a little bit more  complicated and so i think we're headed  to a little bit more of a disordered  world as a result  peter zelmay of uh president zielinski  has said that he wants to make sure  there are strong security guarantees in  place before there is any kind of accord  any kind of agreement um who would act  as a guarantor and and how difficult  would that be i mean how tough will it  be  to to find somebody who would be  acceptable to both sides uh various  countries might play a part in this but  how tough would that be to make a  reality and who do you think might be  the leading contenders  very tough it will be very tough uh to  go by the track record vladimir putin  views any uh and every uh round of  negotiation uh as a stepping stone to  greater escalation uh and once again uh  has shown himself less than uh  trustworthy uh we also have the  president of the 1993 budapest  memorandum which uh ukraine was  guaranteed security in uh exchange for  turning over its nuclear is significant  in nuclear arsenal over to russia and  look where has it has gotten us you know  you ukraine voluntarily disarmed itself  and now it's in a very vulnerable  position to say the least so vladimir  zelensky has said that uh very rightly  so through his interlocutor mr arakania  who is his  representative in the parliament that uh  this should be uh a guarantee stronger  than the collective security article of  nato article five uh and currently what  is being considered is that these the  countries that would be guarantors would  be country's members of the current uh  makeup of the security council and the  u.n including  obviously the permanent five so this is  something uh in this sense it's  unprecedented it's a completely  different security architecture but this  just speaks volumes about the kind of  vulnerability uh ukraine finds itself in  and once again the you know untrust  untrustworth untrustworthiness of of  russia you know ukraine has to have a  very very strong guarantees and in that  respect i think uh talk about  demilitarization ukraine is a non-star  ukraine has to have uh you know even  more defensive capability than it ever  has in order for this arrangement to  work  andre cortonov would it be acceptable in  the long term to president putin if  president zielinski were to remain in  power or would the ultimate aim of  president putin be  to see regime change and to have  president zilinski replaced in ukraine  well uh  i don't know what kind of personal  relations  they have  i  might be skeptical about any kind of  personal affinity between the two men  but uh in my opinion uh zelensky is uh  the  the only legitimate leader in ukraine uh  you can probably replace him with  someone else but this someone else  will not have the legitimacy and he or  she will not be accepted by the  ukrainian population  uh and uh what is the alternative i  personally do not see any  and i think that uh gradually we see uh  more and more readiness on the russian  side uh to negotiate with vladimir  zelansky  we don't know what will happen  at the next presidential elections in  ukraine but zelensky has emerged as an  international icon if you wish  he is known all over the world he is  respected and even admired  in many countries so how can you get rid  of him  why should you do that  my take is that uh  russia will have to deal with vladimir  zelinski if russia wants to end  these  this conflict so that's the only way to  go  zachary bacon let me ask you a version  of the same question i mean has from  your point of view president putin  accepted the fact that if he wants to go  forward with this he's going to have to  deal with president zilinski or does he  from your point of view does he want to  replace zelinski  i i think that it increasingly uh looks  like like like it's the case that he has  prepared to tolerate zinski remaining in  power uh but i think that this has a  little bit less to do with just the  dynamic between putin and zelinski  themselves i mean it's been said on this  panel already that you know putin needs  to be able to have you know some sort of  victory that he can sell at home in  order to preserve his own political life  i think this has more to do with just  putin it has to do with russia itself  right russia for centuries has viewed  its identity vis-a-vis europe as being a  great power and being a term setter and  if you can't set the terms vis-a-vis  ukraine you're certainly not able to set  the terms vis-a-vis nato the united  states or the collective west  and so this is a fundamental question  almost one could say an existential  question for russia because it has to do  with you know what does it mean to be  russia  ever since the russian imperial polity  began constructing itself it has in many  ways sought recognition from the west as  a respected equal  and that has been one of the drivers of  russian national identity for a long  time uh so these are some very very  difficult questions that remain  unresolved in the post-cold war era and  that russia is obviously having a very  difficult time wrestling with just like  other empires like the british empire  and the french empire and others also  had difficulty adjusting uh to their  novel circumstances uh as well as for  the notion uh you know of these security  guarantees that are currently being  discussed for ukraine i think it's  deeply unfortunate that we had to have a  war  in order to come to a situation where  we're discussing a perfectly reasonable  albeit difficult and tricky but  nonetheless a reasonable proposal for  how to revise the european security  architecture or at least revise the  architecture as it relates to ukraine  and other countries that want to have a  guaranteed neutral status  you know i think it was pretty clear to  any honest observer that mindless nato  expansion had outlived its usefulness as  a fundamental pillar uh of the post-cold  war european security order simply  because it had been rejected by russia  and if you don't have the buy-in of the  continent's most powerful country you're  not going to have a stable or durable  order on the continent so i i take the  idea i take the notion that these  proposals are being discussed right now  simply the fact that they're being  discussed as a positive indication that  maybe in the future there will be the  opportunity to pursue more creative  diplomacy and avoid future crises  peter zelmayev you and i on this program  in the past have discussed how important  it is for ukrainians that ukraine be  able to try and join nato at some point  so  if ukraine  now says that it is going to adopt a  neutral status  in exchange for security guarantees if  that is something that is acceptable to  president zielinski how does he sell  that to the public is that something  they would be willing to accept  that's a very good question you know  there's still much appetite among the  ukrainian public uh  for even nato membership but this is  where you know politicians come in and  they have to make choices that are  at odds with the will of the population  the previous speaker said something  about you know i made a point about  nato's mindless expansion you know that  is a a serious topic meriting a whole  separate discussion you know uh i would  just say that uh for vladimir putin the  nato expansion issue has been a red  herring i think what we're seeing in  ukraine is nothing to do very little to  do with nato expansion to ukraine  considering that you know  nato was not coming to ukraine in the  next  few years probably enough of vladimir  putin to you know uh finish out this  term in his life from we're talking  about 10-15 years uh rather what we're  seeing is that all 19th century style  land grab land conquest uh that vladimir  putin has been perpetrating against uh  ukraine and so uh i think vladimir putin  the vladimir lenski voldemort is gonna  have to make a tough choice he's gonna  he said he's gonna submit this to the  referendum and i think it's gonna be up  to him and his team to explain to the  ukrainians the kind of compromises and  sacrifices they're gonna have to make  all right we have run out of time so  we're gonna have to leave the  conversation there thanks so much to all  of our guests peter zelmayev andre  cortonov and zachary pakin  and thank you two for watching you can  see the program again anytime by  visiting our website aljazeera.com and  for further discussion go to our  facebook page that's facebook.com  forward slash aj inside story you can  also join the conversation on twitter  our handle is at aj inside story for me  mohammed jim jerome the whole team here  bye for now  you
 

